1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:12,270
This is the lawyer. It reads the patent landscape around a molecule or a method, and it rules on whether a project may move or publish governed data, always from the instrument.

2
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:23,456
Its file lists the work. Search the patent databases for genes, molecules and devices, and summarize the claims in plain language a scientist can act on.

3
00:00:23,456 --> 00:00:37,069
Rule on whether governed data may move, by reading the agreement, never by assuming it. And triage every referral first, so a question with no IP surface costs one step.

4
00:00:37,449 --> 00:00:48,209
It is not a substitute for counsel of record: a binding opinion goes through the Research and Innovation Office. It does not do the science, and it does not file or prosecute.

5
00:00:48,209 --> 00:00:58,061
And it always separates fact from optimism: a status is confirmed across databases, or it is inferred, and it says which.

6
00:00:58,449 --> 00:01:06,051
A real run, on a synthetic question: can we publish a protocol note and post our script under an open licence?

7
00:01:06,051 --> 00:01:20,286
The verdict is Clear, after triage alone. Disclosure is not an act a patent reaches, so no database was searched, and the answer says so. It adds the one clock the lab should know about.

8
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:35,267
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the lawyer whether you can act on a molecule, or whether a data move is permitted. Say what you intend to do; a landscape for its own sake is not a question.

9
00:01:35,267 --> 00:01:51,731
The first line is the verdict: Clear, Conflict or Unknown. Then what it searched, or why triage said nothing needed searching. The adversary also sends it work on its own during an audit.
